						<h2 id=3D"toc-hId-1795117094">Introduction</h2>
<p>As with many microcontrollers, STM32 MCUs could be clocked by an externa=
l source like crystals (XTAL) or crystal oscillators (XO). This is in addit=
ion to internal clocks such as LSI, HSI, MSI, CSI etc. For <strong>crystals=
</strong>, a preliminary calculation is needed to determine if it is suitab=
le for a specific STM32 MCU.</p>
<p>This article is intended for new STM32 users. It shows by examples how t=
o know if the crystal is suitable for your STM32 or not.</p>
<p>And finally, an important point: The best selection of components is onl=
y worth half as much if the corresponding layout does not fit. Therefore, w=
e recommend reading section 5 regarding the layout.</p>
<h2 id=3D"toc-hId--757039867">1. HSE and LSE structures</h2>
<p>The HSE and LSE blocks of STM32 MCUs are based on a Pierce oscillator:</=
p>
<p><span class=3D"lia-inline-image-display-wrapper lia-image-align-center" =
image-alt=3D"Figure 1. Pierce oscillator structure" style=3D"width: 400px;"=
><span class=3D"lia-message-image-wrapper"><img src=3D"https://community.st=
.com/t5/image/serverpage/image-id/103007i93ADA080DBFD0D8B/image-size/medium=
/is-moderation-mode/true?v=3Dv2&amp;px=3D400" role=3D"button" title=3D"Figu=
re 1.png" alt=3D"Figure 1. Pierce oscillator structure" li-image-url=3D"htt=
ps://community.st.com/t5/image/serverpage/image-id/103007i93ADA080DBFD0D8B?=
v=3Dv2" li-image-display-id=3D"'103007i93ADA080DBFD0D8B'" li-message-uid=3D=
"'780236'" li-messages-message-image=3D"true" li-bindable=3D"" class=3D"lia=
-media-image" tabindex=3D"0" li-bypass-lightbox-when-linked=3D"true" li-use=
-hover-links=3D"false" li-compiled=3D"true"><!----></span><span class=3D"li=
a-inline-image-caption">Figure 1. Pierce oscillator structure</span></span>=
</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p class=3D"lia-align-center">&nbsp;</p>
<p class=3D"lia-align-left">Where:</p>
<ul>
<li class=3D"lia-align-left"><font color=3D"#000000"><strong>Inv</strong>: =
the internal inverter that works as an amplifier.</font></li>
<li class=3D"lia-align-left"><font color=3D"#000000"><strong>Q</strong>: cr=
ystal quartz or a ceramic resonator.</font></li>
<li class=3D"lia-align-left"><font color=3D"#000000"><strong>RF</strong>: i=
nternal feedback resistor.</font></li>
<li class=3D"lia-align-left">
<p><font color=3D"#000000"><strong>R<sub>Ext</sub></strong><span>: external=
 resistor to limit the inverter output current and to form a lag network wi=
th C<sub>L2</sub> to add additional phase shift for frequencies of 8 MHz or=
 below.</span></font></p>
</li>
<li class=3D"lia-align-left"><font color=3D"#000000"><strong>C<sub>L1</sub>=
</strong> and <strong>C<sub>L2</sub></strong>: are the two external load ca=
pacitances. Generally, they have the same value.</font></li>
<li class=3D"lia-align-left"><font color=3D"#000000"><strong>C<sub>s</sub><=
/strong>: stray capacitance, sum of the device pin (OSC_IN and OSC_OUT) and=
 the PCB (a parasitic) capacitances. It is the equivalent parallel capacita=
nce seen by the crystal.</font></li>
</ul>
<p>The Pierce Oscillator (was a US patent 2'133'642, filed in 1924, expired=
 in 1955) is known for its stable oscillation in conjunction with a quartz =
crystal. This is why it is by far the most commonly used gate oscillator to=
day.&nbsp;<span data-teams=3D"true">If you want to learn more about how a P=
ierce Oscillator works, we recommend reading, <a href=3D"https://www.st.com=
/resource/en/application_note/an2867-guidelines-for-oscillator-design-on-st=
m8afals-and-stm32-mcusmpus-stmicroelectronics.pdf#page=3D11" target=3D"_sel=
f" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">AN2867</a>, section 3, or this <a href=3D"ht=
tps://www.crystek.com/documents/appnotes/Pierce-GateIntroduction.pdf" targe=
t=3D"_self" rel=3D"nofollow noopener noreferrer">article by Ramon Cerda</a>=
.</span></p>
<p>To start the oscillator, it requires a certain minimum amount of energy.=
 The next sections show how to make some calculation to define if a crystal=
 is suitable for your STM32 (HSE or LSE).</p>
<h2 id=3D"toc-hId-985770468">2. Calculation to validate the crystal compati=
bility with STM32</h2>
<p>To ensure the minimum of energy to let the oscillator start and sustain =
the oscillation, it needs to meet this condition:<span class=3D"lia-inline-=
image-display-wrapper lia-image-align-center" image-alt=3D"mALLEm_0-1741348=
529814.png" style=3D"width: 400px;"><span class=3D"lia-message-image-wrappe=
r"><img src=3D"https://community.st.com/t5/image/serverpage/image-id/103008=
